<title>UK: Chinese Boeing 737 insured for $42 million - broker.</title>
<headline>Chinese Boeing 737 insured for $42 million - broker.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-05-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>A Boeing 737-300 which crashed last Thursday at Huangtian airport, southern China, had hull insurance of $42 million, broker Willis Corroon told Reuters.</p>
<p>London market sources said the lead underwriter was John Butler of Lloyd's of London's Murray Lawrence syndicate 824. No-one was available to comment at the aviation syndicate.</p>
<p>The aircraft was delivered new in February, 1994 to China Southern Airlines. Willis and Bowring are brokers to the airline, a Willis spokesman said.</p>
<p>The Boeing was totally destroyed after being diverted to Huangtian in bad weather. It broke in two pieces on landing and caught fire at 2130 local time on May 8.</p>
<p>Two of the nine crew and 33 of the 65 passengers have been confirmed dead.</p>
<p>-- John Sanders, Reuters Insurance News, +44 171 542 2769</p>
</text>
